    ['Cleave', '/kliːv/', 'm', 'To split, divide, or sever something forcibly with a sharp weapon; also (ironically!) to cling closely to.', 'From OE "clēofan" (to split, past tense "clove" or "cleft").', '"With one mighty stroke of his axe, the woodsman clove the knotty oak log in twain."', 'Chaucer, "Knight\'s Tale"'],
